<h2>How to choose</h2>
<h3>Check your car first</h3>
<p>Most roof racks and boxes need crossbars. If your car has factory side rails, you may only need crossbars and towers made to fit them; bare roofs need a fit kit. Use the rack maker's fit guide for your exact make, model, and year, and check your car's roof load limit in the owner's manual. The combined weight of bars, box, and gear must stay under it.</p>
<h3>Capacity</h3>
<p>Ski racks are rated by how many pairs of skis or snowboards they hold. Snowboards with bindings take more room than skis. For roof boxes, check the interior length against your longest skis and the volume against what you plan to carry.</p>
<h3>Height clearance</h3>
<p>A roof box can add significant height. Measure your total height before entering garages, parking structures, and drive-throughs. Many people put a reminder note on the dash.</p>
<h3>Security and access</h3>
<p>Look for locks on clamps or lids. Roof boxes that open from both sides are easier to load in tight parking spots.</p>
<h3>Road conditions</h3>
<p>Road salt and spray are hard on exposed skis and bindings. A box or a ski bag inside the car protects them. Rinse racks and gear after salty drives.</p>

<h2>Driving tips</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Load skis tips-back on roof racks to reduce lift and wind noise.</li>
  <li>Double-check that clamps and lids are locked before driving.</li>
  <li>Expect more wind noise and slightly higher fuel use with a box or rack.</li>
  <li>Remove empty racks or boxes when you're not using them.</li>
</ul>
<p>Planning the drive? See <%= link_to "how to plan a ski trip", guide_path("how-to-plan-a-ski-trip") %> and pack boots in a <%= link_to "ski boot bag", guide_path("ski-boot-bag") %>. Browse more <%= link_to "gear guides", guide_topic_path("gear") %>.</p>

